abstract: >-
  A research compendium on justifiable diagnosis, intervention and routing
  recommendations under explicit assumptions for multi-RAT smart-grid
  communication networks, where private LTE and 900 MHz radio reach the same
  grid sites over a fibre core. Its central question is whether, without a
  labelled fault dataset, a diagnosis-plan-route chain can be both auditable
  and verifiable. Experiment 001 answers it for a single incident: a rule-based
  expert system with 41 production rules, MYCIN-style certainty factors, forward
  and backward chaining and why/how explanation; STRIPS planning solved by GPS
  means-ends analysis and by A* progression search; and A* routing with a
  straight-line heuristic proven admissible and consistent and checked against
  Floyd-Warshall. Experiment 002 extends it to the whole network with a
  blackboard of eleven knowledge sources: rule experts split from the same base
  without changing any single-node conclusion, an incident correlator that
  explains simultaneous outages by one upstream cause, an A* access router and
  a multi-RAT arbiter deciding medium switch, lost redundancy or isolation per
  site, and a planner ranking repairs by damage. Experiment 003 adds
  eco-resolution agents with an exhaustive convergence analysis, and
  experiment 004 builds the network in ns-3 with private LTE in band 31,
  injects faults and checks the recommended failover in simulation. All
  scenarios are synthetic and all thresholds nominal and uncalibrated.
